# features.d/alpine-fde.modules — mkinitfs MODULE list for the alpine-fde
# Early-Boot Unseal Hook (docs/Architecture.md §8.2; ADR-13; real-server
# blocker #14). mkinitfs packs kernel modules ONLY from <feature>.modules:
# entries are resolved against /lib/modules/<kver>/ and expanded through the
# modules.dep dependency closure (sbin/mkinitfs find_kmods) — the .files
# route cannot carry modules (its ldtree pass drops every non-ELF file).
#
# `alpine-fde install` stages this file next to alpine-fde.files and the
# step-7 host record APPENDS the target's resolved module keys here (found
# under <mnt>/lib/modules/*/kernel, with the /lib/modules/<kver>/ prefix
# stripped — modules.dep key shape), so a kernel that moves module paths
# still packs. mkinitfs dedupes the closure (sort -u) — re-runs are safe.
#
# Directory entries pack the whole subtree: the TPM dir carries tpm.ko CORE
# plus EVERY interface driver the kernel ships (tpm_tis/tpm_crb/…), which is
# what the audit's ANY-of interface-driver requirement resolves against
# (blocker #12 refinement).
kernel/drivers/char/tpm
kernel/fs/efivarfs
kernel/fs/btrfs
kernel/fs/ext4
kernel/fs/jbd2
kernel/fs/mbcache
kernel/drivers/md/bcache
# USB + HID stack (R640 2026-10-01): the iDRAC virtual keyboard is a USB HID
# device — without these subtrees it never enumerates in the initramfs and
# the recovery passphrase is UNTYPEABLE at the video console (the recovery
# read is /dev/console; on the default UKI that is the virtual console).
# Directory entries pack the whole subtree through the modules.dep closure,
# same mechanism as the TPM entry above.
kernel/drivers/hid
kernel/drivers/usb/host
kernel/drivers/usb/common
